DATABASE_URL=postgres://uthavu:uthavu@localhost:5433/uthavu_dev
REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6380
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=
BETTER_AUTH_URL=http://localhost:3001
PORT=3001

# msg91 — see docs/decisions/0006-otp-via-msg91-from-the-start.md
MSG91_AUTH_KEY=
MSG91_TEMPLATE_ID=

# --- FCM push notifications ------------------------------------------------

# Firebase Cloud Messaging, the only notification channel this product has
# (CLAUDE.md § App Profile: push = fcm, email = none). Alerts are written to the
# `alerts` table AND pushed to every device the recipient registered via
# POST /devices — see src/push/.
#
# LEAVE BOTH BLANK and the API falls back to DevConsolePushProvider, which logs
# the notification it would have sent (`docker compose logs -f api`) instead of
# calling Firebase. Same pattern, and the same reasoning, as the msg91 OTP
# fallback above: the feature stays testable end to end before the credentials
# exist. Set both and FcmPushProvider takes over automatically — no code change.
#
# The fallback is HARD-BLOCKED in production: with NODE_ENV=production and
# either value missing, the API refuses to start (src/push/push-provider.factory.ts).
# A push that silently goes nowhere is worse than a crash — push is how a nearby
# volunteer finds out an emergency request exists at all.
#
# FCM_PROJECT_ID must equal the `project_id` inside the service account JSON;
# a mismatch is rejected at startup rather than failing every send at runtime.
FCM_PROJECT_ID=

# The full service account JSON from the Firebase console
# (Project settings -> Service accounts -> Generate new private key), either as
# raw JSON on one line or base64-encoded. Base64 is usually easier: the
# `private_key` field contains literal newlines that most .env parsers mangle.
#   base64 -i service-account.json | tr -d '\n'
FCM_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON=

# --- Admin console ---------------------------------------------------------

# Origin of the admin console (apps/admin), which runs on port 3002 — see its
# package.json (`next dev -p 3002`). Do NOT point this at 3000; that port hosts
# a stale prototype from a different checkout.
#
# It must match the console's origin EXACTLY — scheme, host and port. A wildcard
# is not an option: admin auth is cookie-based, so responses carry
# `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`, and browsers reject `*` in
# `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` on a credentialed request. A mismatched port is
# a different origin and is refused just like a different host.
#
# The value feeds two independent checks, both of which need it:
#   - the CORS allowlist in src/main.ts (app.enableCors)
#   - Better Auth's own `trustedOrigins` in src/auth/auth.ts, which is its
#     cross-site request defence, not a CORS setting
#
# Leave it unset and the console cannot log in. Mobile is unaffected either way:
# it sends a Bearer token with no Origin header and no cookie, so CORS never
# applies to it.
ADMIN_URL=http://localhost:3002

# Passwords for the two accounts `pnpm db:seed` provisions
# (admin@uthavu.org = super_admin, ops@uthavu.org = ops_admin). Left blank they
# fall back to the development defaults from docs/webadmin/01-admin-login.md
# (Admin@123 / Ops@123) — which the seed REFUSES to use when NODE_ENV=production,
# along with anything shorter than 12 characters.
SEED_ADMIN_PASSWORD=
SEED_OPS_PASSWORD=

# By default the seed creates an admin's password once and never touches it
# again, so re-running it (the API container runs `db:migrate && db:seed`-adjacent
# startup) can't undo a rotation. Set this to `true` for one run to force the
# seeded passwords back to the values above. There is no self-service password
# reset: /api/auth/forget-password returns 400 RESET_PASSWORD_DISABLED because
# this project has no email provider (docs/decisions/0003-no-email-provider-at-launch.md).
SEED_ADMIN_FORCE_PASSWORD_RESET=

# --- Photo uploads ---------------------------------------------------------

# The public origin that uploaded photos are served from — scheme + host + port,
# no trailing path (e.g. https://cdn.uthavu.app, or http://192.168.1.5:3001 when
# testing on a real device over the LAN).
#
# SET THIS AND EVERY STORED URL BECOMES UNIFORM. Photos live on the API's own
# disk (ADR 0008) and `POST /uploads` returns an absolute URL that is then
# persisted — into `report_photos.url`, `mission_completions.photo_url` and
# `user.avatar_url` — and rendered as-is by the mobile app. With this unset, the
# API has to infer the origin from the incoming request, which means the origin
# recorded in a row is an accident of *who uploaded it* (curl on the host, a
# phone on wifi, a proxy) rather than a fact about where the file lives. Setting
# it removes the inference, and with it a whole class of problem: rows that
# disagree with each other, rows that are unreachable from another network, and
# — the reason this comment exists — a caller-supplied `Host` header ending up
# in a column that citizen devices fetch from (see src/uploads/upload-url.ts).
#
# LEAVE IT BLANK and the API uses the request's own Host, but only when that
# host is one of the origins below; anything else falls back to BETTER_AUTH_URL
# rather than being stored. That is safe, but it is a fallback — a photo
# uploaded through an undeclared proxy gets a URL that does not resolve from
# every network. Set this before anything but a single-machine dev setup.
#
# SAFE TO SET NOW. It used to have a trap: `MissionsService.isGenuineUpload()`
# verified a completion photo against a hard-coded `${BETTER_AUTH_URL}/uploads/`
# prefix, so pointing uploads anywhere else refused every mission completion.
# That predicate now lives in src/uploads/stored-upload.ts and resolves its
# accepted origins from this variable, BETTER_AUTH_URL and EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL
# together — the same declaration upload-url.ts builds URLs from, so the check
# and the generator cannot disagree. Rows written before you set this keep
# working, because their origin is still declared.
UPLOADS_PUBLIC_URL=

# Where report photos wait while moderation decides about them.
#
# MUST NOT BE INSIDE THE PUBLIC UPLOADS DIRECTORY. main.ts mounts UPLOADS_DIR as
# Express static under /uploads/, outside every Nest guard, so anything written
# there is world-readable the instant it lands and cannot be un-published. A
# photo awaiting a verdict therefore lives somewhere the static middleware is not
# mounted, and reaches a moderator only through an authenticated controller.
#
# The API refuses to start if this resolves inside UPLOADS_DIR — a silent
# "quarantine" that is actually serving files is worse than a crash, so it is
# fatal rather than a warning (same reasoning as the FCM production block).
#
# Blank uses <cwd>/uploads-pending, a sibling of the public directory. In Docker
# that is /repo/apps/api/uploads-pending, mounted as the named volume
# `uthavu_api_quarantine`.
#
# THAT VOLUME IS REQUIRED, NOT OPTIONAL. Without it this directory lives on the
# container's writable layer and every rebuild destroys every photo awaiting
# moderation while its photo_uploads row survives — leaving a queue whose items
# a moderator can neither view nor approve. Observed before the fix: 30 held
# rows, 3 files on disk. Any deployment target other than docker-compose.yml
# must give this path durable storage of its own.
QUARANTINE_DIR=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# QUARANTINE RETENTION (how long held/refused photos stay on disk)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Quarantined photos used to accumulate forever: discardQuarantined() existed and
# nothing ever called it. That is a storage leak and a privacy problem — §34 asks
# for a minimum retention period and the deletion of rejected files, and a
# photograph refused for nudity or gore has no remaining use.
#
# THE ONE RULE THAT OUTRANKS EVERY VALUE BELOW: deleting the file never deletes
# the record. The `photo_uploads` row — verdict, reasons, risk level, reviewer,
# timestamps — is the accountability trail and survives the bytes. Nothing in the
# sweep writes to that table.
#
# A PHOTO AWAITING A MODERATOR IS NEVER DELETED, at any age. Its retention clock
# does not start until `reviewed_at` is set, because the file IS the evidence the
# moderator is about to look at.

# Days a quarantined file is kept after its clock starts — the moderator's
# decision if there was one, otherwise verification.
#
# 30 is the shortest window that still outlasts the things that come back for the
# picture: an appeal from a citizen whose photo was refused, a moderator
# revisiting their own call, a safety report that arrives days later. Set it too
# LOW and a legitimate dispute finds the evidence already gone. Set it too HIGH
# and the product is holding refused imagery for no reason it can name, which is
# the thing the policy exists to prevent. Blank or unparseable falls back to 30.
QUARANTINE_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# Minutes between sweeps. 0 DISABLES CLEANUP ENTIRELY (files then accumulate
# forever again — that is the failure this whole block exists to end).
#
# There is no cron and no queue in this API, and on Vercel a promise left
# floating after the response is killed with the invocation. So the sweep runs
# opportunistically on the photo-upload request, throttled through a Redis key
# (`uploads:quarantine-sweep`) whose TTL is this value — at most one sweep per
# window across every instance, however many uploads arrive at once. REDIS_URL is
# therefore required for cleanup to happen at all; a Redis outage skips the sweep
# rather than failing the upload, and deletion resumes on the next window.
#
# Six hours is chosen against COST, not urgency: nothing here has a deadline
# measured in hours. Much shorter adds latency to more uploads for no privacy
# gain; much longer lets a backlog build faster than one capped batch can drain.
QUARANTINE_SWEEP_INTERVAL_MINUTES=360

# Ceiling on files deleted per sweep.
#
# The sweep runs inside a citizen's upload request, so its worst case is somebody
# waiting during an emergency. The cap turns "the first sweep after a long
# backlog unlinks fifty thousand files" into "it unlinks 200, and the next sweep
# takes the next 200". Set it too LOW and a backlog drains slower than it grows
# (200 every 6h is 800/day — comfortably above any realistic upload rate here);
# set it very HIGH and you have removed the only bound on how long one unlucky
# upload waits.
QUARANTINE_SWEEP_BATCH=200

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# IMAGE MODERATION (report photos)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# WITHOUT THESE, NOTHING IS APPROVED AUTOMATICALLY. An unconfigured provider does
# NOT wave photos through — it reports that it analysed nothing, and every report
# photo is routed to the admin review queue instead. That is deliberate: a photo
# nothing has examined is not a safe photo, and "we could not check" must never
# collapse into "we checked and it was fine".
#
# The API REFUSES TO START if these are missing while NODE_ENV=production, for
# the same reason the FCM block exists: silently holding every report is a
# failure an operator would experience as "the app is broken", with nothing
# anywhere naming the cause.
#
# `rekognition` (default) or `none`. `none` is honoured outside production only,
# and still routes everything to review rather than approving it.
AI_MODERATION_PROVIDER=rekognition

# Required. The SDK's default credential chain can find an instance role or a
# shared profile, but it cannot guess a region — so this is the one value that
# decides whether moderation is considered configured at all.
AWS_REGION=

# Optional. Leave BOTH blank to use an IAM instance role or a shared profile,
# which is the better production shape. Setting only one of the pair is worse
# than setting neither: it overrides the default chain with something unusable.
# Needs rekognition:DetectModerationLabels and rekognition:DetectLabels.
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# Deadline for the pair of Rekognition calls, which run concurrently — so this
# is the slower one, not their sum. A breach routes the photo to review.
AI_MODERATION_TIMEOUT_MS=8000

# MinConfidence sent to DetectModerationLabels. Rekognition's own default is 50.
AI_MODERATION_MIN_CONFIDENCE=50

# ── Thresholds (0-100 confidence unless noted) ───────────────────────────────
# All optional; the defaults shown are what the code uses when blank. Tune from
# what the review queue actually fills up with, not from a guess.
#
# REJECT — no human sees these, so the bar is high.
MODERATION_REJECT_EXPLICIT=80
MODERATION_REJECT_HATE_SYMBOLS=80
# Both sharpness AND brightness must fall below this. A dark-but-sharp night
# photo still publishes: night is when help is needed.
MODERATION_REJECT_UNUSABLE_QUALITY=12
#
# REVIEW — a moderator decides.
MODERATION_REVIEW_NON_EXPLICIT_NUDITY=60
MODERATION_REVIEW_SWIMWEAR=75
MODERATION_REVIEW_GRAPHIC_VIOLENCE=80
# THE EMERGENCY CARVE-OUT. Uthavu carries photographs of injured animals and
# accident scenes. Rekognition returns the whole ancestor chain, so blood at 95
# also reports Graphic Violence at 95 — judging on the parent alone would hold
# every injury photo the product exists to deliver. When the ONLY child label
# that fired is `Blood & Gore`, this higher bar applies instead. Set it equal to
# MODERATION_REVIEW_GRAPHIC_VIOLENCE to switch the carve-out off.
MODERATION_REVIEW_GRAPHIC_VIOLENCE_GORE_ONLY=92
MODERATION_REVIEW_VISUALLY_DISTURBING=70
# Signal only; never an accusation.
MODERATION_REVIEW_WEAPONS=70
# Never causes a REJECT at any confidence. Rekognition's drug taxonomy bottoms
# out at `Pills` and `Smoking`, which cannot tell prescription medication at a
# crash site from anything illicit.
MODERATION_REVIEW_DRUGS=70
# "This is a cartoon / a drawing." NOT AI-generation detection — Rekognition
# offers none, and nothing in this system claims otherwise.
MODERATION_REVIEW_NON_PHOTOGRAPHIC=80
# Confidence a scene label needs before it counts toward category relevance.
MODERATION_SCENE_LABEL_CONFIDENCE=55
# Perceptual-hash Hamming distance (0-64 bits) treated as a near-duplicate.
MODERATION_DUPLICATE_DISTANCE=8

# The API origin the MOBILE build is compiled against (apps/mobile/.env). It is
# duplicated here, not just in apps/mobile, because the API trusts it in two
# places: Better Auth's `trustedOrigins` (src/auth/auth.ts) and the upload-URL
# host check above — a genuine upload from the phone arrives with exactly this
# host, so leaving it unset is what makes on-device photos fall back to
# BETTER_AUTH_URL (i.e. to `localhost`, which on a phone means the phone).
# Keep the two files in step; when testing on a device, both hold the LAN
# address, not localhost. Setting it is also what lets a photo taken on that
# phone be ACCEPTED: a report photo or a completion photo uploaded there carries
# this origin, and src/uploads/stored-upload.ts refuses any origin no variable
# here declares.
EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:3001

# --- Rate limiting ---------------------------------------------------------

# EVERY VARIABLE IN THIS SECTION IS OPTIONAL AND EVERY DEFAULT IS SAFE. Leaving
# the whole block unset gives a working, documented limit on every endpoint —
# never an open door. The numbers live in src/rate-limit/rate-limit-config.ts,
# which carries the reasoning for each one; this file only says how to override.

# How many reverse proxies sit between this API and the public internet.
#
# THE MOST DANGEROUS VALUE IN THIS FILE. It decides whether every IP-keyed limit
# is real or decorative, because it decides which address Express believes.
#
#   0 (the default)  X-Forwarded-For is ignored entirely; the client IP is the
#                    socket address. Cannot be spoofed. Behind a proxy it makes
#                    every user look like the proxy, so limits over-count.
#   1                Correct on Vercel — one hop. Express counts hops from the
#                    socket end and reads X-Forwarded-For RIGHT TO LEFT, so it
#                    lands on the address Vercel observed, and a client that
#                    prepends its own entries only pushes them out of reach.
#
# NEVER set this to `true`. Express reads that as "the client is the LEFT-most
# X-Forwarded-For entry" — the entry the client itself wrote — which lets anyone
# mint unlimited fresh rate-limit buckets with a header. Any non-integer value
# (including `true`) falls back to 0 rather than widening trust.
#
# It defaults to 0 rather than 1 because an unset variable must fail in the
# direction that cannot be exploited: over-counting is visible within minutes of
# a deploy, while a spoofable limiter is discovered by the incident it failed to
# prevent. SET THIS TO 1 ON VERCEL.
#
# Note it also affects req.protocol and req.hostname. Nothing in this codebase
# regresses as a result — src/uploads/upload-url.ts reads x-forwarded-proto
# itself and validates the Host against a declared allowlist either way — but
# it is worth knowing before changing the value.
TRUST_PROXY_HOPS=0

# Per-policy overrides: RATE_LIMIT_<POLICY>_MAX and RATE_LIMIT_<POLICY>_WINDOW
# (window in seconds). A value that is not a positive integer — including 0 —
# falls back to the documented default rather than being adopted, because
# RATE_LIMIT_READ_MAX=0 almost certainly means "unlimited" and would instead
# have refused every read in the product.
#
# The defaults, and what each one governs:
#
#   ip-global      600 / 60s    Every request, keyed on IP, BEFORE authentication.
#                               Deliberately generous: Indian mobile networks are
#                               heavily CGNAT'd, so one address can be thousands
#                               of unrelated subscribers. Its job is to bound
#                               anonymous floods and identity-cycling, not to be
#                               precise. Tighten it on non-CGNAT infrastructure.
#   auth           100 / 300s   /api/auth/* per IP. A BACKSTOP behind the precise
#                               controls (3 OTP sends per PHONE per 10 min, and
#                               Better Auth's 5 verify attempts), which are the
#                               ones that work correctly under CGNAT.
#   report-create    5 / 600s   POST /reports. The tightest limit in the product:
#                               a report pushes a notification to every nearby
#                               volunteer, so spam here is strangers' phones.
#   mission-chat    60 / 300s   POST /reports/:id/messages. Sized from what a
#                               person can type on a phone under stress.
#   comment         20 / 300s   Public Community Comments.
#   support-ticket  10 / 3600s  Tickets and replies — the scarce resource is the
#                               human reading them.
#   avatar-upload   20 / 900s   POST /uploads. Every call writes a file to the
#                               API host's own disk (ADR 0008).
#   write           30 / 60s    Default for any undecorated citizen mutation.
#   read           120 / 60s    Default for citizen reads. Generous because
#                               mobile POLLS — there is no realtime transport.
#   admin-write     60 / 60s    Any POST/PATCH/PUT/DELETE under /admin/*.
#   admin-read     240 / 60s    Any GET under /admin/*. The console is dense and
#                               a page load fans out to several endpoints.
#
# GET / (health) is exempt from all of it: a liveness probe that gets a 429 is
# reported as an unhealthy instance, so a limiter there can roll back a good
# deployment by itself.
#
# Two limits are NOT in this table and are not configurable here, deliberately:
# the 3-sends-per-phone OTP limit (src/auth/otp/otp-rate-limiter.ts, a documented
# product rule from docs/features/auth.md BR-2) and the 20-per-15-minutes photo
# upload ceiling (src/uploads/upload-rate-limiter.ts, sized to a Rekognition
# bill). Both are business rules rather than traffic shaping, and both fail
# CLOSED on a Redis outage where everything above fails open.
# RATE_LIMIT_REPORT_CREATE_MAX=5
# RATE_LIMIT_REPORT_CREATE_WINDOW=600

# The kill switch, for an incident where the limiter itself is the problem.
# Named in the negative so the safe state is what you get when nothing is set:
# only the exact string `true` disables enforcement, and no typo in any limit
# above can. MUST NEVER BE SET IN PRODUCTION — the API logs a warning at startup
# when it is.
# RATE_LIMIT_DISABLED=true
